# Remaining UX concerns

**1. Per-item materiality is now invisible, and the skip copy is uniform.** Every skip reads the same: *may need to be provided later before the workflow can make certain decisions.* Skipping the variance tolerance breaks every pass and hold determination in the SOP. Skipping decision-trail granularity changes nothing anyone will notice. Both now produce identical guidance. A user optimizing for speed will skip by convenience — shortest question, least familiar topic — rather than by consequence, and the interface no longer distinguishes. The information survives, but only in the artifact, after the decision. This is the largest cost of removing the `If skipped:` lines and I would want to watch for it.

**2. `Open:` is the only remaining signal about closure, and it is one-directional.** A user can see what the compiler still wants. They cannot see what the compiler decided it already knows. If it wrongly reads a partial answer as complete, that item silently leaves `Open:` and the user's only chance to catch it is the Assumptions Ledger inside the compiled artifact — post-hoc. Removing the resolved list removed the pre-compile audit surface entirely. Correct per your instruction, still a real loss, and it puts weight on the ledger being read.

**3. The `Recorded:` echo is an addition you did not ask for.** I added it because §3.8's letter grammar has a genuine collision risk and a misparsed selection is otherwise undetectable until compile. If you find it noisy, deleting §3.9 is clean and costs nothing structurally — but then a misparse of `1C` as `1C` when the user meant something else surfaces only in the artifact.

**4. `D. I'll provide my own answer` is honest and weak.** It reduces the *appearance* of a closed set. It does not much reduce anchoring, because it is the highest-effort option on the list by a wide margin — three lettered answers cost one keystroke, and the escape costs a sentence. Users take the cheap path. Keeping options simplest-first and short is mitigation, not a fix; the shape of an answer is still being suggested by whatever three examples I chose.

**5. Grouped questions concentrate load behind a low keystroke count.** Q4 in the reference render is one question carrying a reviewer, four refusals, and an authority level — six decisions, twelve characters. Typing effort is minimal; reading effort is not. The three-question cap no longer bounds the number of decisions per turn, only the number of lines. If users start under-reading groups, the lever is the six-element limit in §3.5, tightened.

**6. `Reason:` is doing more work than two sentences comfortably hold.** It now carries what the old per-pillar reason strings and the old consequence lines carried between them. When three unrelated items are open — a reviewer, a format, a routing rule — one honest sentence covering all three tends toward the generic (*these affect how decisions are made*), which is close to no information. It will read well when the open items share a cause and poorly when they do not.

**7. Fast mode is unchanged and still gets none of this.** Every improvement in this phase lands on `/slow`. A `/fast` user's first sight of what was unresolved remains the gate list inside the artifact, in full internal vocabulary. If fast is the common invocation, the presentation layer improved the less-travelled path.
